"""Practical skills consolidation; organisation added by YYeni, no extra syllabus."""
LESSONS={}
def lesson(oid,title,text,cards):LESSONS['OBJ-6117-'+oid]={'title':title,'text':text,'cards':cards}
lesson('12.1-01','Reading scales with appropriate precision', '''A measurement needs a quantity, value and unit. Before reading a scale, identify its direction and the value represented by one small interval. A thermometer usually increases upwards, whereas a burette increases downwards because it records volume delivered from its upper zero. Read an aqueous liquid's lower meniscus at eye level to avoid parallax. Record enough precision to reflect the scale and method, without adding unsupported digits.

For a burette, volume delivered is final reading minus initial reading. If the readings are 1.20 and 24.80 cm³, the delivered volume is 23.60 cm³. Adding the readings gives a number without the required physical meaning. A negative result in an ordinary delivery experiment should prompt a check of reading order. Two readings both have measurement uncertainty, so a very small delivered volume may have a relatively large percentage uncertainty. Instrument readings are evidence; changing a recorded reading to match an expected answer is not legitimate. If a mistake is found, retain a clear correction rather than silently manufacturing agreement.''',[
('Describe how to read and record an aqueous burette level reliably.','Read the bottom of the meniscus at eye level, then record the value with the burette unit and suitable precision.',['Eye-level lower-meniscus reading (1).','cm³ and precision appropriate to scale (1).']),
('State the thermometer reading in this scale description: labelled marks 60 and 65 °C have five equal intervals, and the liquid ends at the third interval above 60.','63 °C.',['63 °C (1).']),
('Explain the error in adding burette readings 1.20 and 24.80 cm³ to obtain a titre of 26.00 cm³.','A titre is the increase in reading, so subtract initial from final: 24.80 − 1.20 = 23.60 cm³. The addition is the error.',['Correct subtraction and 23.60 cm³ distinguish volume delivered from sum (1).'])])
lesson('12.1-02','Interpolating between divisions', '''Interpolation estimates a value between nearby marked values. First determine the size of an interval from two numbered marks and the number of spaces between them. If 20 and 30 cm³ are separated by ten equal intervals, each interval is 1 cm³. A liquid level halfway between 24 and 25 cm³ is about 24.5 cm³, provided the drawing or instrument supports that estimate.

Count spaces rather than tick marks. Including both end marks in the count can give the wrong interval size. An estimate between divisions is not a licence to report many decimal places. The useful precision depends on the clarity of the scale, the width of the meniscus or pointer and the instrument. If a diagram is only schematic and has no readable scale, do not infer a precise value from its size. The visual panels for this topic have specified divisions so readings can be checked. Compare upward and downward scales explicitly: a lower meniscus position on a burette corresponds to a larger reading, while a higher liquid column on a thermometer corresponds to a higher temperature.''',[
('Describe how to estimate a reading halfway between the 24 and 25 cm³ marks on a clear scale.','Identify the 1 cm³ interval and estimate its midpoint as 24.5 cm³.',['Correct interval size (1).','Midpoint estimate 24.5 cm³ (1).']),
('State the reading halfway between 18.0 and 18.2 cm³ on a burette scale.','18.1 cm³, commonly recorded as 18.10 cm³ with consistent burette precision.',['18.1/18.10 cm³ (1).'])])
lesson('12.1-03','Repeats and anomalous results', '''Repeated measurements help reveal random variation and allow a more reliable estimate of a result. Keep the method and controlled variables the same. Results that agree closely can be averaged where an average is meaningful. A markedly different reading should be investigated; it must not be deleted merely because it is inconvenient or differs from a prediction.

For original practice data, titres 20.10, 20.20 and 20.10 cm³ are close together, while 23.80 cm³ warrants checking and a repeat. If a specific procedural error is documented for the outlier, explain why it is excluded from a final average. Repetition does not automatically remove systematic error: a miscalibrated instrument can give very similar but biased results. Distinguish repeatability from accuracy. Also distinguish a preliminary rough trial from carefully repeated measurements when the method calls for that distinction. Record all relevant results, then justify the processing decision. More decimal places in the mean do not necessarily represent greater experimental precision.''',[
('Describe how repeated measurements help assess a practical result and one limitation.','They reveal variation and support a representative mean, but do not remove a systematic calibration error.',['Variation/reliability or justified mean (1).','Systematic error can remain (1).']),
('State which reading most needs investigation in repeated titres 20.10, 20.20, 23.80 and 20.10 cm³.','23.80 cm³.',['Identify the anomalous 23.80 cm³ reading (1).'])])
lesson('12.2-01','Explaining an arrangement', '''An apparatus arrangement should make the intended measurement possible and control relevant conditions. To measure gas production, a reaction vessel can be connected through a bung and delivery tube to a freely moving gas syringe. Connections must prevent unintended leakage while allowing gas into the measuring device. A sealed reaction vessel with no expansion or outlet is not an appropriate substitute.

Explain the role of each feature. A thermometer bulb should contact the liquid being measured without touching the hot vessel wall. A condenser's cooling water enters at the lower connection and leaves at the upper connection so the jacket remains filled. A lid in a cooling investigation helps standardise evaporation and heat loss, but must allow the intended thermometer arrangement. General phrases such as 'make it accurate' are weaker than naming the error being reduced. An experimental arrangement also includes timing: define when the reaction starts and when the measurement is taken. For a rate comparison, a delayed bung or timer can lose early gas and bias the measured rate. Link the diagram to the procedure rather than treating it as decorative. In an acid-rate comparison, specify acid identity and concentration: strong/weak describes ionisation, whereas concentrated/dilute describes amount per volume. Changing both at once confounds the comparison.''',[
('Describe an arrangement to measure gas volume during an acid–carbonate reaction.','Connect the reaction flask through a well-fitting bung and delivery tube to a freely moving graduated gas syringe.',['Connected flask/bung/tube arrangement preventing unintended leakage (1).','Graduated gas syringe with movable plunger/outlet capacity (1).']),
('Explain the fault in a cooling setup whose thermometer bulb touches the hot glass wall rather than remaining within the water.','The reading may reflect the wall temperature instead of the bulk water temperature.',['Wrong thermal contact biases intended water measurement (1).'])])
lesson('12.2-02','Tables and numerical processing', '''A results table should identify each quantity and place its unit in the heading. Record raw measurements before calculating derived values. For mass loss, subtract later mass from earlier mass; for burette delivery, subtract initial reading from final reading. The direction follows the physical quantity being requested, not a universal rule to subtract the smaller printed number from the larger without thought.

Original data: a reaction vessel falls from 104.36 g to 103.92 g, so 0.44 g has left the measured system. If gas escape is the intended cause, the result can represent mass of gas lost under suitable controls, but evaporation or splashing could also affect the measurement. A table column headed 'mass lost / g' is different from 'mass / g'. Keep units consistent during processing and show the formula or relationship. A calculator output should be rounded to a precision justified by the input data. Processed data do not replace raw readings; retaining both allows another person to check the subtraction and interpret a possible error.''',[
('Complete the mass-loss entry: initial mass 104.36 g; later mass 103.92 g; mass lost = ____.','0.44 g.',['104.36 − 103.92 = 0.44 g (1).']),
('State the volume delivered from a burette whose initial and final readings are 2.30 and 19.70 cm³.','17.40 cm³.',['Final minus initial gives 17.40 cm³ (1).']),
('Explain the error in recording 208.28 g as the mass lost when readings change from 104.36 to 103.92 g.','The readings were added; the correct mass loss is the decrease, 104.36 − 103.92 = 0.44 g.',['Reject addition and correctly identify the 0.44 g decrease (1).'])])
lesson('12.2-03','Drawing usable apparatus diagrams', '''A scientific apparatus diagram shows the functional connections and positions clearly. Simple two-dimensional lines are usually more useful than decorative perspective. Label the vessel, measuring device and relevant liquid or solid. A gas-delivery tube must connect to the gas space or intended reaction arrangement and lead into the collection device without an unexplained break. A label line should end at the component it names.

For filtration, show a funnel containing filter paper above a receiving vessel. The residue remains on the paper and the filtrate passes through. A soluble product is not recovered from the residue merely because the residue is easier to see. For a thermometer in a cooling experiment, show the bulb immersed but clear of the vessel wall and bottom. Diagrams need not reproduce manufacturing details, but they must not imply impossible flow through solid glass. When evaluating a supplied diagram, name the specific missing connection or incorrect position and its consequence. A neat drawing can still be scientifically wrong, while a simple correct schematic can communicate the method well.''',[
('Draw a labelled filtration arrangement for separating an insoluble solid from its liquid.','A funnel lined with filter paper sits above a receiving vessel; solid residue stays on the paper and filtrate collects below.',['Funnel with filter paper above receiver (1).','Residue and filtrate correctly labelled (1).']),
('State the missing component in this described filtration diagram: a bare funnel holds a suspension above a beaker, and solid passes through with the liquid.','Filter paper or an appropriate filter medium.',['Filter medium inside funnel (1).'])])
lesson('12.2-04','Choosing apparatus for a purpose', '''Choose apparatus by the quantity, range and precision required. A volumetric pipette is suitable for transferring one specified volume accurately. A burette delivers a measured variable volume, such as during titration. A measuring cylinder is useful for many less precise volume measurements, while an ordinary beaker is usually unsuitable for a precise titre. A balance measures mass, not volume.

The reason should connect the device to the task. Saying that a pipette is 'better' is incomplete without stating the fixed accurate transfer it provides. The instrument range must also fit the quantity: a device that cannot contain the required amount is unsuitable even if its scale is fine. Select a thermometer with an appropriate range and division size for a cooling investigation. A stopwatch is useful for timed observations but does not measure temperature. For gas measurements, consider whether the gas is soluble or reactive in a collection liquid before choosing displacement over water. The choice belongs to the chemistry and measurement requirements together, not to habit.''',[
('Describe suitable apparatus for transferring exactly 25.0 cm³ solution to a titration flask and explain the choice.','Use a 25.0 cm³ volumetric pipette with a pipette filler; it is designed for an accurate fixed-volume transfer.',['Volumetric pipette/filler (1).','Accurate fixed-volume reason (1).']),
('State the more suitable choice for delivering a measured variable acid volume: a burette or an ungraduated beaker.','A burette.',['Burette for controlled measured delivery (1).'])])
lesson('12.3-01','Conclusions supported by data', '''A conclusion should answer the investigation question using the observations actually obtained. Quote a relevant comparison, trend or calculated value with units. Original cooling results at ten minutes are 50 °C without insulation and 63 °C with insulation, from the same 80 °C starting temperature. The insulated vessel lost less temperature under the stated conditions, supporting better insulation in that test.

Do not claim more than the design establishes. If vessel size or water mass also changed, the result cannot isolate insulation material. If only one trial was made, acknowledge that variation has not been measured. A result consistent with a prediction supports it but does not prove it in every possible setting. 'The graph goes down' is an observation; 'temperature falls as time increases under these conditions' is a more precise trend, and a justified comparison needs the relevant values. Avoid making up a reading outside the measured range. Where differences are small compared with measurement uncertainty, a cautious conclusion may be more appropriate than a definite ranking.''',[
('Describe a conclusion supported by equal-start cooling results of 50 °C uninsulated and 63 °C insulated after ten minutes.','The insulated vessel cools less over ten minutes, retaining a temperature 13 °C higher under the matched conditions.',['Insulated vessel loses less heat/temperature under conditions (1).','Uses 63 versus 50 °C or 13 °C difference (1).']),
('State the supported conclusion if sample A produces 30 cm³ gas in 20 s and B produces 12 cm³ in the same time under matched conditions.','A has the greater average gas-production rate over that interval.',['Greater rate for A over stated interval (1).'])])
lesson('12.3-02','Observation, inference and limitations', '''An observation records what is seen or measured; an inference explains what it may mean. 'A white precipitate formed' is an observation. 'The sample may contain chloride' is an inference that requires the reagent and test conditions. A result cannot be interpreted reliably without knowing the procedure. Colour, physical state and behaviour on adding excess reagent may all matter.

Evaluate whether the data distinguish the proposed alternatives. A white precipitate with sodium hydroxide alone is not enough to choose between several cations. Additional observations, such as solubility in excess ammonia, can narrow the possibilities. Similarly, a gas volume difference does not prove a different amount of reactant if temperature, pressure or leakage changed. In this course numerical gas tasks use their stated room-condition relationships; practical comparisons still require matched conditions. A cautious conclusion identifies what is supported and what remains unresolved. Do not replace a missing observation with the expected one. If an outcome conflicts with a prediction, report it and investigate the method or explanation.''',[
('Describe the difference between the observation “white precipitate” and an ion identification.','The precipitate is what is seen; identification is an inference that also needs the reagent and any further distinguishing observations.',['Observation distinguished from inference (1).','Conditions/additional evidence needed for identification (1).']),
('State a limitation of identifying a cation from “white precipitate with sodium hydroxide” alone.','Several cations can give that observation, so it is not a unique identification.',['Non-unique result / further test needed (1).'])])
lesson('12.3-03','Plotting and interpreting graphs', '''A graph should label both axes with quantities and units, use an even suitable scale and place points accurately. Time usually belongs on the horizontal axis in a cooling experiment. Choose a scale that uses the available grid well while covering the data. The plotted line should represent the relationship: a cooling curve is usually a smooth decreasing curve, whereas a justified linear relationship uses a straight best-fit line. Do not force every noisy point into a zigzag merely to connect them.

For DATA-12, time in minutes is 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 and uninsulated temperature in °C is 80, 72, 65, 59, 54, 50. The insulated series is 80, 76, 72, 69, 66, 63. The graph shows slower cooling with insulation under matched conditions. Read between nearby data points only within the supported range, and state a reasonable tolerance. A curve should not swing above or below the trend because of an unsuitable fitting method. Marking separates axes/scales, point accuracy and the line or curve. A correct answer read from a badly labelled graph does not repair the missing axis information.''',[
('Describe two essential features of a graph of temperature against time from a cooling experiment.','Label axes as time/min and temperature/°C with even suitable scales; plot the measured points accurately and draw a suitable cooling curve.',['Labelled quantities/units and usable even scales (1).','Accurate points with suitable curve (1).']),
('State the approximate temperature at 5 min on a straight interpolation between 65 °C at 4 min and 59 °C at 6 min.','62 °C.',['Midpoint interpolation 62 °C (1).'])])
lesson('12.3-04','Gradient, intercept and intersection', '''Gradient measures change in the vertical quantity divided by change in the horizontal quantity. A temperature–time graph therefore has gradient in °C/min when those are the axis units. For a straight segment from (2 min, 72 °C) to (6 min, 59 °C), the average gradient is (59 − 72)/(6 − 2) = −3.25 °C/min. The negative sign means temperature falls. A cooling-rate magnitude can be reported as 3.25 °C/min if the question asks how quickly temperature decreases.

Use a large appropriate triangle on a best-fit line when finding its gradient; nearby points amplify reading uncertainty. For a curved graph, distinguish an average rate over an interval from the instantaneous rate represented by a tangent. The vertical intercept is the value where the graph crosses the vertical axis, often the initial value when time is zero. An intersection represents equal vertical values at the same horizontal value. Those meanings depend on the axes, so do not state simply 'the answer is where lines meet' without naming the physical quantity. An extrapolated intersection outside the data is less secure than an observed one.''',[
('Determine the average gradient between (2 min, 72 °C) and (6 min, 59 °C) on a temperature–time graph.','−3.25 °C/min.',['(59 − 72)/(6 − 2) = −3.25 °C/min (1).']),
('State the physical meaning of an intersection of two temperature–time curves at 4 min and 60 °C.','Both samples have temperature 60 °C at 4 min.',['Same temperature at the stated time (1).'])])
lesson('12.4-01','Specific errors and improvements', '''An improvement should address a named source of error. If gas escapes while a stopper is being fitted, some early product is missed; a suitable closed starting arrangement can reduce this loss. If a temperature probe touches the container, reposition it in the liquid. 'Be more careful' is not a method change unless the action and error are specified.

Random variation and systematic error require different responses. Repeats help assess random scatter, while calibration or correction is needed for a consistent instrument offset. Increasing sample size can reduce some relative uncertainties but may introduce new practical constraints. A proposed improvement must remain feasible and safe. Do not choose a more precise instrument if its range is unsuitable or if a much larger uncontrolled error dominates. Explain the chain: source of error, effect on the measured result, and procedural change. When the direction of bias is not known, say so rather than guessing that every error makes the result too low.''',[
('Identify a procedural improvement when gas is lost before a bung is fitted at the start of a reaction.','Start the reaction within an already connected collection arrangement, for example by releasing one reactant after closure.',['Feasible closed-start method reducing early gas loss (1).']),
('State why repeating readings from a balance with a constant +0.20 g offset does not remove that offset.','Each repeat has the same systematic error; calibration or correction is needed.',['Systematic bias persists through repetition (1).'])])
lesson('12.4-02','Precautions with reasons', '''A precaution is useful when its purpose is clear. Reading a meniscus at eye level reduces parallax. Keeping a thermometer bulb off the vessel wall makes it more representative of the liquid temperature. Wearing eye protection reduces injury risk from splashes, but it does not by itself improve numerical accuracy. Distinguish safety precautions from measurement controls, even when both belong in the method.

Evaluate a proposed improvement against the actual task. A lid can reduce evaporation and make cooling comparisons more controlled, but identical lid arrangements must be used for both samples. A stopper must not create a sealed pressure hazard in a gas-producing reaction with no outlet. Controlled heating and a heatproof mat address hot-apparatus risks, while constant starting temperature addresses fairness. The best response is specific and causal: state the action and explain the hazard or error it reduces. A long list of generic laboratory rules does not substitute for the one relevant precaution asked for.''',[
('Explain why the lower meniscus should be read at eye level.','It reduces parallax error from viewing the level at an angle.',['Parallax reduction (1).']),
('Explain why adding a lid to only one vessel would weaken a comparison intended to isolate insulation-jacket material.','The lid also changes heat loss, introducing a second differing variable.',['Confounding effect of unequal lids (1).'])])
lesson('12.5-01','Planning a fair investigation', '''An investigation plan needs a testable question, a justified prediction, an independent variable, a measured dependent variable and controlled conditions. Name suitable apparatus and explain the sequence well enough for another person to follow. State quantities or a justified range rather than saying 'some water'. Define timing and the endpoint, include repeats, and explain how results will support a conclusion.

For comparing insulation, vary the jacket material while keeping vessel, water amount, initial temperature, jacket thickness and surrounding conditions matched. Record temperature at fixed times, compare cooling curves and repeat the trials. Predict less temperature loss for a better insulator. A six-mark plan should contain distinct method, control, measurement and interpretation points, not six rephrasings of 'make it fair'. A marked cross in a clouding experiment has a different role: it sits beneath the flask as a visual endpoint marker. It is not chromatography paper used to separate the mixture.''',[
('Plan a fair comparison of two insulation materials using cooling water. Include the measurements and conclusion method.','Wrap identical vessels with equal-thickness jackets of the two materials and add equal water volumes at the same starting temperature. Keep lids and surroundings matched. Record temperature at fixed time intervals with correctly positioned thermometers, repeat trials, and compare temperature drops or cooling curves; the smaller loss indicates better insulation under those conditions.',['Same vessel and water volume (1).','Same starting temperature (1).','Material varied with thickness/lids controlled (1).','Temperature measured at fixed times (1).','Repeats with matched surroundings (1).','Compare cooling to draw a conditional conclusion (1).']),
('State the uncontrolled variable in a plan that compares equal fabric jackets but starts one water sample at 80 °C and the other at 60 °C.','Starting water temperature.',['Unequal initial temperature (1).']),
('Explain the error in placing marked-cross paper inside a clouding reaction as though it were chromatography paper.','The cross belongs beneath the flask; time until the developing cloudiness hides it when viewed from above. It marks the endpoint rather than separating components.',['Correct placement and endpoint role distinguish it from chromatography (1).'])])
lesson('12.6-01','Reagent, observation and inference', '''A qualitative test response should name the reagent or procedure, state the observation and give a conclusion limited by that evidence. For hydrogen, a lighted splint gives a squeaky pop; oxygen relights a glowing splint. Carbon dioxide turns limewater milky. Confusing the splint conditions changes the test, so 'a splint' alone is incomplete.

Ion tests often need observations both before and after adding excess reagent. Copper(II) with aqueous ammonia can first form a light-blue precipitate that dissolves in excess to give a deep-blue solution. The final colour belongs to a solution, not a precipitate. Use fresh portions for separate tests and avoid contamination. The complete test tables are taught in Topic 8. This consolidation chapter organises the syllabus practical skills rather than adding a twelfth content topic to the official eleven; the linked practical index points back to all 55 suggested experiences in their teaching contexts.''',[
('Describe the test for carbon dioxide and its positive observation.','Pass the gas through limewater; it becomes milky because a precipitate forms.',['Limewater test (1).','Milky/cloudy observation (1).']),
('State the supported ion conclusion from a light-blue precipitate with ammonia that dissolves in excess to a deep-blue solution.','Copper(II) ions are indicated.',['Cu2+ / copper(II) (1).'])])
